1
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:29,600
We're broadcasting from our campus in Silicon Valley, California, standing outside Google's very first data center, built inside a shipping container.

2
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:32,560
There's an undeniable nostalgia to this place.

3
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:33,360
Welcome.

4
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:37,920
I'm Amin Vadat, and I lead the AI and infrastructure organization here at Google.

5
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:50,948
More than 25 years ago, we built our first data center on cork boards and commodity chips with a radical belief that software could coordinate thousands of machines to effectively create a supercomputer.

6
00:00:51,502 --> 00:00:56,932
We use that supercomputer to organize the world's information and make it universally accessible and useful.

7
00:00:57,550 --> 00:01:03,428
That architecture-powered search, it indexed the web and laid the foundation for modern cloud computing.

8
00:01:03,982 --> 00:01:10,468
It's an incredible foundation we stand on as we look over the threshold to an even more profound inflection point.

9
00:01:10,926 --> 00:01:21,028
Whether you are joining us from a watch party in Tokyo, London, or New York, or streaming from your desk, it is truly a privilege to speak with you at such an exciting time for technology.

10
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:24,640
We are witnessing breakthroughs almost every week.

11
00:01:25,038 --> 00:01:30,020
A few weeks back, we announced Gemini's evolution into a universal AI agent.

12
00:01:30,542 --> 00:01:38,468
You give it an objective, it spins up sub-agents, queries your systems, coordinates tools, and works across multi-day tasks.

13
00:01:39,054 --> 00:01:49,092
To power that intelligence, earlier this year, we announced our eighth generation TPU systems, TPU 8T for training, and TPU 8i for real-time inference.

14
00:01:49,518 --> 00:01:54,116
Usually, when tech companies say their chips are out of this world, it's hyperbole.

15
00:01:54,638 --> 00:01:56,708
In this case, it's a statement of fact.

16
00:01:57,262 --> 00:02:10,720
Last month, with our project Suncatcher Moonshot, we launched TPUs into low Earth orbit aboard a prototype satellite, exploring how near constant solar energy can one day power the machine learning workloads of the future.

17
00:02:11,182 --> 00:02:13,348
Here's Maria Biggs to tell us more.

18
00:02:13,934 --> 00:02:14,560
Thanks, Amin.

19
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:19,300
Hi everyone, my name is Maria, and I'm the engineering director for Project Suncatcher.

20
00:02:19,822 --> 00:02:25,120
Four weeks into this early orbit prototype test, we're reminded why this moonshot matters.

21
00:02:25,390 --> 00:02:28,800
The sun is the ultimate power source in our solar system.

22
00:02:29,198 --> 00:02:36,000
Turning this audacious idea into workable reality requires disciplined, step-by-step experimentation.

23
00:02:36,398 --> 00:02:42,788
Last month, we were excited to take our first major step by deploying our prototype satellite into orbit.

24
00:02:43,374 --> 00:02:46,948
Shortly after launch, our team confirmed contact with the satellite.

25
00:02:47,342 --> 00:02:55,524
Since then, we have powered the host and TPU trays and have successfully run early machine learning tests on these TPUs in orbit.

26
00:02:56,160 --> 00:03:08,260
In fact, we also recently had our first Hello World milestone in space, sending a prompt to the satellite, running the inference on the TPUs, and verifying the output back here on Earth.

27
00:03:08,910 --> 00:03:19,844
These early flight experiments are helping us gather critical data on how our hardware handles extreme environments like launch, intense cosmic radiation, and thermal management in vacuum.

28
00:03:20,622 --> 00:03:23,680
Everything we learn here paves the way for what's ahead.

29
00:03:23,982 --> 00:03:28,260
Launching a pair of satellites next year to test inner satellite laser links.

30
00:03:28,846 --> 00:03:32,640
There's a lot more to come from Project Suncatcher, so watch this space.

31
00:03:33,070 --> 00:03:35,200
Back to you, Amin, for today's updates.

32
00:03:35,630 --> 00:03:36,708
Thank you, Maria.

33
00:03:37,326 --> 00:03:43,524
Together, our platform and Silicon Advancements tell the story of an industry undergoing profound change.

34
00:03:44,206 --> 00:03:59,332
Now, the unprecedented growth in computing demands spurred by agents is creating a fundamental inversion across three dimensions as we move first from human scale to agent scale, second, from systems of record to systems of action.

35
00:03:59,630 --> 00:04:04,292
And third, from simple requests to complex agentic workflows.

36
00:04:05,006 --> 00:04:10,640
To understand what that means for technology, consider what it takes to power today's applications.

37
00:04:11,278 --> 00:04:16,640
When you type a query into Google Search, it coordinates across 10,000 servers in hundreds of milliseconds.

38
00:04:16,910 --> 00:04:19,364
It returns an answer and its job is complete.

39
00:04:19,950 --> 00:04:22,080
An agent presents unique challenges.

40
00:04:22,382 --> 00:04:25,732
It receives an objective, thinks, then acts.

41
00:04:26,382 --> 00:04:33,840
It decomposes its goal, writes code, queries a model, calls external tools, inspects its output, and self corrects.

42
00:04:34,798 --> 00:04:39,940
When a human interacts with AI, they might exchange a thousand tokens in a brief back and forth.

43
00:04:40,366 --> 00:04:51,684
An agent executing that same task turns inference into an ongoing loop, triggering dozens of subagents, firing hundreds of model queries, and consuming millions of tokens.

44
00:04:52,462 --> 00:04:57,952
Over the past year alone, agent-driven traffic surged by more than 7,800%.

45
00:04:59,118 --> 00:05:07,780
Today, inference makes up somewhere between 50 and 70% of all AI compute consumption, up from 40% just two years ago.

46
00:05:08,462 --> 00:05:10,112
And this is the beginning of the curve.

47
00:05:10,446 --> 00:05:21,060
Recent industry forecasts project that global token demand will multiply 24 times by 2030, reaching over 120 quadrillion tokens every month.

48
00:05:21,518 --> 00:05:26,020
If you do the math, that's a 55-fold surge in compute intensity.

49
00:05:26,830 --> 00:05:40,432
To make modern AI economically viable, your data center facilities, compute provisioning, networking, data storage, silicon, runtimes, and frameworks need to be optimized from the ground up.

50
00:05:40,814 --> 00:05:51,460
At Google Cloud, we call it the AI hypercomputer, a co-designed system open at every layer, powered by data centers with industry-leading energy efficiency.

51
00:05:52,398 --> 00:05:54,180
What does co-design mean in practice?

52
00:05:54,446 --> 00:06:04,352
It means precision engineering inside each individual layer and optimizing across layers so the entire cluster operates as a single, unified system.

53
00:06:05,390 --> 00:06:07,588
Let's start with the silicon layer as an example.

54
00:06:08,238 --> 00:06:15,812
Today, all 14 of our 1 billion user products are using Gemini models, which are powered by TPUs.

55
00:06:16,366 --> 00:06:22,180
It's tempting to imagine that the scale was the result of some flawless multi-year master plan.

56
00:06:22,798 --> 00:06:26,692
The truth is, hardware breakthroughs rarely start with a grand vision.

57
00:06:27,150 --> 00:06:31,588
They start when you push a system to its absolute limit and something breaks.

58
00:06:32,910 --> 00:06:39,392
For TPUs, that moment came in 2013 when we deployed early neural networks on Google Voice Search.

59
00:06:39,662 --> 00:06:47,940
We realized that if every Android user spoke to their phone for just three minutes a day, we would have had to literally double the number of Google data centers.

60
00:06:48,526 --> 00:06:49,392
Two Googles.

61
00:06:49,710 --> 00:06:50,756
That was impossible.

62
00:06:51,214 --> 00:06:59,364
So we asked the fundamental question: what if we built a domain-specific processor for matrix mathematics, the language of neural networks?

63
00:07:00,270 --> 00:07:07,076
The TPU was born, and it gave us 30 times the throughput of comparable processors of that time.

64
00:07:07,790 --> 00:07:17,892
Since then, over more than a decade, we've designed and deployed eight generations of custom silicon, answering a new scaling bottleneck with every generation.

65
00:07:18,510 --> 00:07:21,072
So what truly defines the anatomy of a TPU?

66
00:07:21,232 --> 00:07:23,940
It comes down to six fundamental principles.

67
00:07:24,590 --> 00:07:36,228
Instead of hardware managing thousands of tiny threads, we use fewer, larger cores so our software compiler can plan out execution without thread schedulers or dynamic branching logic.

68
00:07:37,262 --> 00:07:50,180
Then we use systolic arrays, which work by streaming data continuously through a physical grid of multipliers and reusing it cycle after cycle instead of fetching it from registers for every operation.

69
00:07:51,182 --> 00:07:58,820
Rather than relying on hardware caches, we use compiler controlled memory to schedule exactly when data moves for fewer latency spikes.

70
00:08:00,206 --> 00:08:10,468
Optical circuit switches give us a way to connect thousands of chips using beams of light reflected by tiny mirrors, avoiding the power draw and dropped packets of electrical switches.

71
00:08:11,822 --> 00:08:25,220
We prioritize range over precision using compact number formats like BFloat 16 and native FP4 to execute more math per watt of energy while maintaining strict bitwise determinism.

72
00:08:26,286 --> 00:08:37,156
And our liquid cooling cold plates sit alongside the silicon to prevent chips from overheating so we can pack more compute into each rack and run at peak speed continuously.

73
00:08:38,062 --> 00:08:42,180
These principles are how TPUs deliver incredible price performance.

74
00:08:43,086 --> 00:08:45,508
Let's step into the lab to see the real thing.

75
00:08:48,302 --> 00:08:51,696
On my left is our original TPU V1 from 2015.

76
00:08:51,886 --> 00:08:53,296
It almost looks cute today.

77
00:08:53,614 --> 00:08:59,652
When it was on its board, it was air cooled with a 75-watt PCIe card we built in just 15 months.

78
00:09:00,814 --> 00:09:01,616
Now look at this.

79
00:09:01,902 --> 00:09:06,468
This is our eighth generation TPU 8T board engineered for Frontier model training.

80
00:09:07,054 --> 00:09:10,336
We removed the primary cold plates so you can see the packages.

81
00:09:10,670 --> 00:09:12,900
Right in the center are the two compute chips.

82
00:09:13,262 --> 00:09:16,996
Everything else on this tray exists to keep them running at peak utilization.

83
00:09:17,678 --> 00:09:20,096
Look above them at the heat sinks and capacitors.

84
00:09:20,430 --> 00:09:26,660
Feeding two multi-die packages and 12 memory stacks requires heavy copper power planes inside the board.

85
00:09:27,214 --> 00:09:31,588
Stepping down voltage directly beside the silicon so power is never a bottleneck.

86
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:36,036
Blanking each chip are six stacks of high bandwidth memory.

87
00:09:36,494 --> 00:09:38,788
You can just about see them on the packages.

88
00:09:39,598 --> 00:09:42,596
12 HPM3 stacks on a single board.

89
00:09:43,150 --> 00:09:48,576
Joining them through the board is our interchip interconnect that will connect at the bottom over here.

90
00:09:48,750 --> 00:09:56,964
A multidimensional copper ring running at 19.2 terabits per second bidirectional per chip, double our previous generation.

91
00:09:57,774 --> 00:10:05,732
In the data center, massive liquid cooling plates clamp directly across these packages to provide the highest levels of performance and power efficiency.

92
00:10:06,318 --> 00:10:12,164
We truly set out to maximize performance delivered per provisioned and consumed watt.

93
00:10:13,038 --> 00:10:22,372
And out at the bottom edge, where copper transitions to light for our optical circuit switches, for the first time, the optical transceivers themselves are also liquid-cooled.

94
00:10:22,894 --> 00:10:25,540
You can see the copper cooling assembly right here.

95
00:10:27,918 --> 00:10:30,788
At these power densities, even the light needs a cold plate.

96
00:10:31,502 --> 00:10:46,884
When you scale the stray out to a 9,600-chip superpod, it becomes a training powerhouse, delivering nearly three times higher compute performance per pod, cutting training timelines from months to weeks at 2.7 times lower cost.

97
00:10:47,758 --> 00:10:50,256
But most organizations aren't building new models.

98
00:10:50,510 --> 00:10:54,628
And that brings us to the thinking part of an agentic stack, inference.

99
00:10:55,310 --> 00:10:58,052
This is what TPU 8i was built to solve.

100
00:11:00,046 --> 00:11:02,896
For agents, response speed is everything.

101
00:11:03,246 --> 00:11:09,188
When they're calling a model, processors spend valuable cycles idling while model weights and KV caches load.

102
00:11:10,446 --> 00:11:13,188
How we solve this comes down to networking.

103
00:11:13,806 --> 00:11:23,268
On TP-AT, we utilize a 3D torus because it's exceptionally efficient for pre-training, where models communicate across predictable, synchronous loops.

104
00:11:24,270 --> 00:11:30,500
But data moving across a torus hops neighbor to neighbor, taking up to 16 hops to traverse the cluster.

105
00:11:31,182 --> 00:11:33,860
In agentic inference, that delay compounds.

106
00:11:35,694 --> 00:11:43,204
When a model is distributed across multiple chips, the processors must coordinate partial sums for every single token generated.

107
00:11:43,662 --> 00:11:49,636
If that exchange takes 16 hops across a network, latency grows and the agent stutters.

108
00:11:50,222 --> 00:11:53,444
That is why for TPU8i, we engineered Boardfly.

109
00:11:53,710 --> 00:11:57,456
Boardfly is a hierarchical low diameter mesh topology.

110
00:11:57,616 --> 00:12:09,764
Instead of routing through rings of neighboring chips, Boardfly provides direct high-speed shortcuts across boards and racks, slashing our worst-case network diameter from 16 hops down to just seven.

111
00:12:10,510 --> 00:12:19,396
In other words, we cut interchip communication latency by up to half, keeping token generation fast and uninterrupted.

112
00:12:20,270 --> 00:12:23,620
And at the silicon level, TPU8i breaks the memory wall.

113
00:12:24,302 --> 00:12:41,028
To reduce core idle time during long context decoding, we expanded its high bandwidth memory by 50% to 288 gigabytes and packed three times more SRAM onto each chip to host larger KV caches on the silicon itself.

114
00:12:42,158 --> 00:12:47,556
The result is up to 80% better performance per dollar for low latency serving.

115
00:12:48,046 --> 00:12:51,908
And here we see a modern Javan's paradox play out in real time.

116
00:12:52,526 --> 00:12:58,116
When you cut the cost of intelligence, human ambition doesn't stay fixed, it expands.

117
00:12:58,670 --> 00:13:03,140
Enterprises shift from simple queries to continuous multi agent workflows.

118
00:13:04,622 --> 00:13:12,484
As customers move these autonomous agents into large-scale production, the demand for specialized agentic inference is accelerating.

119
00:13:12,942 --> 00:13:16,612
Right now, production workloads are hitting three distinct bottlenecks.

120
00:13:17,326 --> 00:13:20,976
First, the enterprise trust and RL bottleneck.

121
00:13:21,216 --> 00:13:26,020
Regulated enterprises require hardware-enforced encryption for sensitive data and use.

122
00:13:26,670 --> 00:13:35,332
Meanwhile, AI builders need unified clusters that can run both inference and reinforcement learning, or RL, on the exact same silicon.

123
00:13:36,654 --> 00:13:39,776
Second, the context and agent density bottleneck.

124
00:13:39,936 --> 00:13:47,492
As agents reason across million token contexts and run in parallel swarms, memory bandwidth becomes the primary bottleneck.

125
00:13:47,950 --> 00:13:50,468
Customers are forced into a terrible trade-off.

126
00:13:50,894 --> 00:13:57,156
Truncate the context, run fewer concurrent agents, or accept multi-second latency stalls.

127
00:13:57,774 --> 00:14:00,324
Third, the implementation bottleneck.

128
00:14:00,622 --> 00:14:05,988
To get the most value out of accelerators, they have to be compatible with the software your teams already use.

129
00:14:06,382 --> 00:14:14,500
That is why we introduced Torch TPU, an integration that allows PyTorch to work natively on TPUs without rewriting your code.

130
00:14:14,990 --> 00:14:23,908
We are now seeing unmodified PyTorch models running on TPUs instantly with performance optimized kernels coming up in weeks, trending to days.

131
00:14:25,006 --> 00:14:37,060
Later on, Mark Lohmeyer will share how Google is designing for openness at every layer of the stack, optimized to serve on open frameworks while preserving your flexibility to use multiple hardware platforms.

132
00:14:37,614 --> 00:14:45,316
At Google, our co-design strategy means our hardware engineers work shoulder to shoulder with our frontier research teams at Google DeepMind.

133
00:14:45,870 --> 00:14:57,796
We saw these bottlenecks emerging early, and because our TPU architecture is modular, it gave us the agility to innovate fast and introduce entirely new solutions purpose-built for these demands.

134
00:14:58,638 --> 00:15:07,012
Today, just six months after we announced TPU 8i and AT, we're doing a second hardware drop within the same generation.

135
00:15:07,630 --> 00:15:12,932
To complement our existing portfolio, I am excited to announce two new systems.

136
00:15:13,454 --> 00:15:24,676
TPU 8TX, optimized for secure and confidential inference, and TPU 8i Max, engineered for high throughput, massive context inference.

137
00:15:25,518 --> 00:15:34,596
Instead of waiting years for a traditional semiconductor cycle, we keep innovating quickly, adapting directly to how workloads are evolving in real time.

138
00:15:35,054 --> 00:15:37,412
Let's explore each of them in more detail.

139
00:15:38,222 --> 00:15:40,180
We'll start with TPU-ATX.

140
00:15:41,230 --> 00:15:45,696
TPU ATX is the third configuration in our eighth generation family.

141
00:15:45,966 --> 00:15:56,004
It shares the silicon base of our AT system, 12.6 petaflops of native FP4 computes, and 216 gigabytes of high bandwidth memory.

142
00:15:56,270 --> 00:16:01,220
To solve the exposure problem, TPU ATX shifts trust from software into silicon.

143
00:16:01,614 --> 00:16:12,816
Working together with custom AMD hosts, the system enforces hardware-level PCIe link encryption, cryptographic attestation, and secure memory enclaves.

144
00:16:13,102 --> 00:16:21,572
That means you can run proprietary models and agents with confidence that your intellectual property and data are locked down at the hardware level.

145
00:16:22,126 --> 00:16:35,536
To ensure that we also accommodate the low latency requirements of agentic workloads, TPU8TX adopts the same network we created for 8i, so you can keep agents moving while reducing multi chip communication bottlenecks.

146
00:16:36,622 --> 00:16:42,180
The result is faster model responses with twice lower latency compared to TPU 1T.

147
00:16:42,862 --> 00:16:47,140
Now let's turn our attention to scaling context without hitting the memory wall.

148
00:16:47,598 --> 00:16:53,156
We built TPU 8i Max with the same low latency chips that power our 8i system.

149
00:16:53,646 --> 00:17:01,572
However, we've doubled the amount of silicon per package, building the most powerful inference system in our TPU portfolio.

150
00:17:02,190 --> 00:17:05,636
TPU 8i Max tackles the memory wall in three ways.

151
00:17:06,158 --> 00:17:09,440
First, let's talk about responsiveness and precision.

152
00:17:10,126 --> 00:17:19,940
By integrating two compute dyes into a single package, we provide 384 gigabytes of HBM, an increase of 33% over 8i.

153
00:17:20,526 --> 00:17:28,228
That stores massive KV caches directly in memory so the agent sees the entire context at once, without truncation.

154
00:17:29,230 --> 00:17:37,156
Next, 17.2 terabytes per second of HBM bandwidth streams that context at twice the speed of 8i.

155
00:17:38,062 --> 00:17:46,980
And to improve latency, we integrated 768 megabytes of ultrafast on-chip SRAM, which is twice the SRAM of 8i.

156
00:17:47,854 --> 00:17:50,180
Second, volume and scale.

157
00:17:50,830 --> 00:18:04,996
To support more concurrent workloads without clogging the pipeline, TPU 8i Max scales compute and memory in lockstep, delivering twice the flops, twice the SRAM, and as I just said, twice the HBM bandwidth of 8i.

158
00:18:05,870 --> 00:18:07,620
Think of it like widening a highway.

159
00:18:08,238 --> 00:18:12,452
You don't need the cars to drive faster, you need more lanes to avoid the traffic jams.

160
00:18:12,782 --> 00:18:14,240
We're not changing the speed limit.

161
00:18:14,480 --> 00:18:17,380
Individual tokens process just as fast as before.

162
00:18:17,774 --> 00:18:23,908
But by doubling our HBM bandwidth to 17 terabytes per second, we've added many new lanes.

163
00:18:24,846 --> 00:18:30,820
Now, more cars, meaning more concurrent agents, can drive through at the exact same time.

164
00:18:31,374 --> 00:18:36,868
This means each agent can access more data, consuming and producing vastly more tokens.

165
00:18:37,486 --> 00:18:48,100
And because our compute cores are fed continuously across the entire multi-chip cluster, you get unprecedented agent density and token volume without hitting the memory wall.

166
00:18:49,422 --> 00:18:51,908
And finally, data center density.

167
00:18:52,174 --> 00:18:56,868
We didn't just make the chips smarter and faster, we made them denser and more efficient.

168
00:18:57,646 --> 00:19:01,200
In semiconductor manufacturing, you run into the retical limits.

169
00:19:01,360 --> 00:19:07,364
You can only print a single monolithic die so large before manufacturing yields collapse.

170
00:19:07,982 --> 00:19:18,692
To shrink the physical and power footprint of massive AI clusters, TPU 8i Max packs more compute into every rack without scaling up infrastructure overhead.

171
00:19:19,694 --> 00:19:31,204
By delivering twice the flops and performance density alongside an integrated multi-chip architecture, data centers can deploy significantly more compute power per square foot and per watt.

172
00:19:31,630 --> 00:19:38,852
Together, these four TPU systems give you a purpose-built accelerator for every stage of an agent's lifecycle.

173
00:19:39,246 --> 00:19:53,760
TPU 8T for scale-up training, TPU 8i for low latency reasoning, TPU 8TX for confidential computing, and TPU 8i Max for massive context windows and the highest compute density per rack.

174
00:19:54,318 --> 00:20:04,804
These TPU systems illustrate the impact of using co designed infrastructure, but the effects of our AI hypercomputer optimizations reach far beyond the edge of a single board.

175
00:20:05,166 --> 00:20:08,960
They extend down to a critical physical resource power.

176
00:20:10,318 --> 00:20:16,964
From the grid to the gates, we design our facilities to provision the maximum useful work for every watt we consume.

177
00:20:17,614 --> 00:20:32,676
Across Google's data centers, that approach has yielded fleet-wide power usage effectiveness of 1.09 on average over the trailing 12 months, which translates to 83% less overhead energy than the industry average.

178
00:20:33,422 --> 00:20:39,780
When you combine that facility efficiency with custom silicon and model optimization, the impact just compounds.

179
00:20:40,302 --> 00:20:46,980
Today, our data centers deliver more than three times more compute performance per unit of energy than five years ago.

180
00:20:47,598 --> 00:20:48,740
What does that mean for you?

181
00:20:49,390 --> 00:20:55,076
It means sustainable unit economics when you scale real-world agent workloads on Google Cloud.

182
00:20:55,918 --> 00:20:57,540
There are really two things happening here.

183
00:20:58,094 --> 00:21:00,900
Below the chip, you're using less power per operation.

184
00:21:01,582 --> 00:21:15,812
And above the chip, you get access to optimized software that is open at every layer because groundbreaking hardware only creates value if developers can actually harness it in the languages and frameworks they already use every day.

185
00:21:16,494 --> 00:21:21,168
To show you how we are opening up the software layers of our stack, let me welcome Mark Lohmeyer.

186
00:21:24,208 --> 00:21:26,048
Thanks, Amin, and hello everyone.

187
00:21:26,288 --> 00:21:29,568
Amin just showed you some truly exciting new TPU systems.

188
00:21:29,870 --> 00:21:31,876
That custom silicon is our foundation.

189
00:21:32,526 --> 00:21:36,676
But you need to combine it with open software to unlock its full value in production.

190
00:21:37,262 --> 00:21:43,076
Co-designed software delivers optimal results, but co-designed must never mean closed.

191
00:21:43,630 --> 00:21:54,948
Because at the end of the day, great hardware only pays off if you get both maximum performance and cost effectiveness at scale in production, and complete flexibility and choice in how you build.

192
00:21:55,406 --> 00:22:01,828
We're backing up that commitment with major new announcements that bring the full power of TPUs to the tools your teams already use.

193
00:22:02,958 --> 00:22:04,964
Let's start with flexibility and choice.

194
00:22:05,710 --> 00:22:19,780
At Google Cloud, that means designing for openness at every layer of the stack, compatibility with your code, optimizations to serve on open inference engines, and the choice to run on whatever accelerator hardware platform is right for you.

195
00:22:20,782 --> 00:22:24,772
Now, the vast majority of AI developers live in PyTorch.

196
00:22:25,582 --> 00:22:32,772
From foundation model training to inference, that's where their code is, that's where their libraries are, and that's how they're used to working.

197
00:22:33,518 --> 00:22:36,132
PyTorch has been optimized for GPUs.

198
00:22:36,750 --> 00:22:45,796
But we also heard loud and clear from our customers that they wanted to leverage the price performance of TPUs while keeping their existing teams, tools, and technologies in place.

199
00:22:46,670 --> 00:22:48,708
So we've completely removed that barrier.

200
00:22:49,230 --> 00:22:53,508
Two weeks ago at the PyTorch conference, we launched Torch TPU.

201
00:22:54,254 --> 00:22:59,780
Built with a PyTorch team at Meta, it makes TPUs a native backend for PyTorch.

202
00:23:00,622 --> 00:23:04,804
That means you can run each workload on the accelerator that best suits it.

203
00:23:05,328 --> 00:23:10,724
And your developers keep their code, their tools, and their best practices.

204
00:23:11,534 --> 00:23:15,492
So now your existing code and models can run on TPUs.

205
00:23:15,968 --> 00:23:22,724
But you also need to ensure that they can be served in production with optimized performance without heavy customization.

206
00:23:23,406 --> 00:23:28,420
The two most popular open source inference engines today are VLLM and SGLang.

207
00:23:28,846 --> 00:23:31,812
And many of our customers use them to serve their models on GPUs.

208
00:23:32,366 --> 00:23:36,004
We wanted to make it just as easy to serve them on TPUs.

209
00:23:37,134 --> 00:23:47,076
So, working together with our ecosystem partners, Infraact and RadixArc, we made native TPU support for VLLM and SGLang available out of the box.

210
00:23:47,854 --> 00:23:54,288
So you can effortlessly serve those models on the open inference engines your teams already use.

211
00:23:54,734 --> 00:23:59,460
Our goal with these optimizations is to make TPU adoption as frictionless as possible.

212
00:24:00,622 --> 00:24:04,324
But our strategy is never to create just a single path.

213
00:24:04,718 --> 00:24:10,692
You need the freedom to choose from multiple accelerator platforms without compromising on performance.

214
00:24:11,854 --> 00:24:20,516
That's why we invest equally in the two leading accelerator platforms in the world, Google TPUs and NVIDIA GPUs.

215
00:24:21,070 --> 00:24:29,828
Two weeks ago at NVIDIA GTC, we announced the general availability of A5X on Google Cloud, powered by NVIDIA Vera Rubin platform.

216
00:24:30,606 --> 00:24:36,004
This means NVIDIA's most powerful GPU platform is available on the AI hypercomputer.

217
00:24:36,942 --> 00:24:47,332
We work closely with NVIDIA to make this possible, integrating their GPU hardware and software platforms with our titanium IPUs, high speed networking, and storage on Google Cloud.

218
00:24:48,270 --> 00:24:54,564
And we've made it available through Google Compute Engine, Kubernetes Engine, and many other Google Cloud services.

219
00:24:55,822 --> 00:25:06,308
And for sovereign workloads, you can now use Google Distributed Cloud powered by NVIDIA Blackwell Platform to bring secure AI directly to your on-prem and air gapped environments.

220
00:25:06,926 --> 00:25:14,628
This full portfolio of TPUs and GPUs gives our customers the freedom to choose the platform that best fits their workload needs.

221
00:25:15,246 --> 00:25:20,292
Now, with the flexibility to build on your terms, the other half of the equation is performance.

222
00:25:20,814 --> 00:25:24,740
Historically, getting the best performance out of your hardware required deep expertise.

223
00:25:25,390 --> 00:25:29,168
But we wanted to make it easy for any developer to make these optimizations.

224
00:25:29,550 --> 00:25:38,916
To prevent chips from sitting idle during external tool calls, human in the loop approvals, or heavy model inference cycles, today we're announcing TPU Sync.

225
00:25:40,046 --> 00:25:49,248
TPU Sync moves working memory off the chip and onto server memory the moment an agent pauses, and then streaming it back again, the second tool responds.

226
00:25:49,646 --> 00:25:52,612
Feedback is received, or the next reasoning cycle begins.

227
00:25:53,198 --> 00:25:59,748
This keeps your accelerators focused entirely on active work and enables highly efficient performance for your inference workloads.

228
00:26:00,494 --> 00:26:05,220
As Amin said earlier, agent traffic is the inverse of human interactions.

229
00:26:05,742 --> 00:26:16,196
Human prompts are short and the answers are long, whereas an agent's input context is massive, carrying system instructions, tool definitions, and its conversation history.

230
00:26:16,718 --> 00:26:20,048
Traditional serving setups simply weren't built for that kind of load.

231
00:26:20,366 --> 00:26:26,692
So if you run both the prompt reading and the token generation on the same chip, they fight for resources and slow everything down.

232
00:26:27,310 --> 00:26:36,452
And if a standard load balancer sprays those requests randomly across your fleet, the chip has to reread and process that prompt and context from scratch every time.

233
00:26:37,582 --> 00:26:49,156
To solve both challenges, we are proud to be a founding contributor to LLMD with an initial release that includes foundational primitives like prefix cache aware routing and disaggregated serving.

234
00:26:50,446 --> 00:27:03,076
A few weeks ago, that project reached a major milestone with the launch of LLMD 1.0, which transitions it into a production-hardened control plane for inference, integrated into GKE inference gateway.

235
00:27:04,270 --> 00:27:11,648
In version 1.0, we bring native TPU support to the open engines you're running in production, including VLLM and SGLang.

236
00:27:12,270 --> 00:27:22,084
By combining prefix routing with TPU sync and three-tier KV cache offloading, LLMD keeps prompt context cached across memory, host CPU, and storage.

237
00:27:22,766 --> 00:27:30,308
This prevents context dropping during those long-running agent loops, delivering ultra-low latency and maximum hardware utilization.

238
00:27:31,854 --> 00:27:40,164
With session-aware routing and fairness-driven flow control in GKE inference gateway, LLMD introduces true multi-tenant quality of service.

239
00:27:40,942 --> 00:27:48,836
This enables massive agent loops and background tasks to share the same hardware without starving real-time interactive applications.

240
00:27:50,126 --> 00:27:58,564
And finally, native Torch TPU support brings VLLM and SGLang directly to cloud TPUs behind a single API endpoint.

241
00:27:59,726 --> 00:28:09,252
Teams can deploy identical PyTorch code and scale pre-fill and decode pools across mixed accelerator fleets of TPUs and GPUs.

242
00:28:09,902 --> 00:28:13,476
So that completes the picture for a Gentic inference on accelerators.

243
00:28:13,998 --> 00:28:23,332
Taken together, this open software stack gives your teams the freedom to build, serve, and optimize models on TPUs using exactly the same tools they already know and love.

244
00:28:23,982 --> 00:28:36,772
They can build in PyTorch or Jax, they can serve with VLM or SGLang, scale out with LLMD, and do all of this across the leading two accelerator platforms in the world, TPUs and GPUs.

245
00:28:37,486 --> 00:28:45,476
And that brings us back to our foundational philosophy across Google Cloud: uncompromising openness at every layer of the stack.

246
00:28:46,318 --> 00:28:52,388
Purpose-built accelerators and open software co-designed to deliver exceptional price performance.

247
00:28:53,006 --> 00:28:55,008
That is how your agents think.

248
00:28:56,398 --> 00:29:00,964
But as we established at the start, for agents, inference is only half of the job.

249
00:29:01,454 --> 00:29:04,292
Models reason, but agents also need to act.

250
00:29:04,654 --> 00:29:06,788
And so CPUs are making a comeback.

251
00:29:07,438 --> 00:29:09,088
But not just CPUs alone.

252
00:29:09,390 --> 00:29:19,972
To scale agentic execution and production, you need a new type of computing stack built specifically for agents, combining optimized software with CPUs and purpose-built hardware.

253
00:29:20,910 --> 00:29:22,148
Let's start with the software.

254
00:29:23,022 --> 00:29:27,428
As you heard from Amin earlier, agents are fundamentally different from any other type of workload.

255
00:29:27,982 --> 00:29:36,580
They're long running, they're stateful, but they also frequently are idle, pausing for seconds or hours while waiting for an external API or a human reply.

256
00:29:37,262 --> 00:29:43,588
Yet when they need to act, they need to respond quickly, scale out massively, and do so cost effectively.

257
00:29:44,686 --> 00:29:48,068
How does that translate into what the infrastructure technically needs to provide?

258
00:29:49,230 --> 00:29:51,684
It comes down to three core capabilities.

259
00:29:52,270 --> 00:29:54,276
The first is isolation and security.

260
00:29:55,406 --> 00:29:58,864
When humans write code, it gets reviewed before it ever hits production.

261
00:29:59,214 --> 00:30:05,604
But when agents execute code on your behalf, you need strictly enforced boundaries around what they can and cannot touch.

262
00:30:06,606 --> 00:30:08,864
The second requirement is instant activation.

263
00:30:09,166 --> 00:30:14,384
When an agent pauses to wait for a tool or human input, you need your compute resources to pause too.

264
00:30:14,734 --> 00:30:17,732
And when they resume, users expect them to start up instantly.

265
00:30:18,670 --> 00:30:19,876
The third is statefulness.

266
00:30:20,174 --> 00:30:26,052
Session context and working files must survive across those pauses so agents pick up exactly where they left off.

267
00:30:27,470 --> 00:30:34,564
Internally at Google, as we began building and scaling our own Gemini agents, we realized that yesterday's computing infrastructure just wouldn't cut it.

268
00:30:35,918 --> 00:30:45,732
So we created an entirely new class of computing infrastructure optimized specifically for the unique requirements of running agents at scale, the Agent Compute Engine.

269
00:30:46,862 --> 00:31:02,052
Agent Compute Engine delivers a purpose-built runtime environment that solves isolation, rapid hibernation, and activation and statefulness in a single unified architecture that can deliver scale with an order of magnitude better efficiency than prior approaches.

270
00:31:03,054 --> 00:31:04,196
Let me walk through what we built.

271
00:31:04,942 --> 00:31:08,932
Starting with multi-layer isolation, which it achieves at three levels.

272
00:31:09,614 --> 00:31:19,620
First, at the kernel level, each sandbox runs as a lightweight virtual machine on cloud hypervisor with its own dedicated guest kernel inside the micro VM and walled off from the host.

273
00:31:21,006 --> 00:31:31,428
Second, at the network layer, our integrated Agent Gateway enforces precise egress policies, including custom domain allow lists that are managed entirely from outside the micro VM.

274
00:31:32,590 --> 00:31:42,084
And third, at the identity layer, your sandboxes hold zero hard-coded credentials, injecting cryptographically verified IDs outside the environment.

275
00:31:42,958 --> 00:31:47,908
Next, it activates instantly, starting agent tasks in the blink of an eye.

276
00:31:48,654 --> 00:31:58,948
Every sandbox comes with root access, attached hyperdiscs for block storage, and support for custom guest images, meaning your existing tools, libraries, and runtime scripts run entirely unchanged.

277
00:31:59,950 --> 00:32:08,484
And for your data, a mounted GCS Fuse directory makes your datasets immediately available right inside that sandbox with zero copying or staging overhead.

278
00:32:09,262 --> 00:32:15,716
Finally, it automatically pauses the micro VM the moment an agent goes quiet, stopping compute consumption on the spot.

279
00:32:16,782 --> 00:32:26,084
But unlike traditional serverless setups that force an agent to reboot from scratch, Agent Compute Engine captures the entire working memory and disk state.

280
00:32:27,086 --> 00:32:37,316
So when the model responds or the user replies, the agent picks up where it left off, keeping its full context intact inside the sandbox without skipping a beat.

281
00:32:37,710 --> 00:32:50,916
This allows us to drive significantly higher density of micro VMs on that same hardware resource, scaling to thousands of VMs per host, ultimately significantly improving the economics of running agents.

282
00:32:52,270 --> 00:33:00,900
And by running Agent Compute Engine with our titanium IPUs, we deliver silicon-rooted defense in-depth built for untrusted AI agents.

283
00:33:01,998 --> 00:33:16,484
Every agent runs inside its own dedicated micro VM governed by a minimal memory-safe Rust hypervisor, while Titanium offloads the networking, storage, and line rate encryption onto dedicated silicon, which is physically separate from the guest compute.

284
00:33:17,646 --> 00:33:31,264
Combined with out-of-band egress enforcement that keeps credentials and cryptographic keys out of guest memory, Agent Compute Engine eliminates entire classes of memory corruption and sandbox escape vulnerabilities by design.

285
00:33:32,206 --> 00:33:36,708
We're putting Agent Compute Engine to work everywhere, starting with our own managed services.

286
00:33:37,710 --> 00:33:46,532
If you're an enterprise leader who's looking to roll out agents, you need a platform that scales across your organization with great performance for your users at a cost that does not scale out of control.

287
00:33:46,894 --> 00:33:50,756
We recently announced Gemini's evolution into a universal agent.

288
00:33:51,150 --> 00:33:57,380
Well, when you use Gemini Agents, Agent Compute Engine is the runtime engine that's running under the hood.

289
00:33:57,870 --> 00:34:08,836
That's why when your team interacts with Gemini, agents start their work instantly, they pause without losing their memory, and they can resume a day, a week, or a month later with that context still fully preserved.

290
00:34:10,446 --> 00:34:14,084
Some of you are also building your own custom agents and platforms of your own.

291
00:34:14,734 --> 00:34:21,828
To support you, Google Cloud provides two secure isolated runtimes, leveraging these same foundational capabilities.

292
00:34:22,318 --> 00:34:30,212
Choosing between them comes down to what matters most to your team: deployment speed and efficiency, or open source and flexibility to deploy across multiple clouds.

293
00:34:31,054 --> 00:34:35,396
The simplest and fastest way to tap into Asian Compute Engine is through our API.

294
00:34:36,014 --> 00:34:42,276
You can quickly create, pause, and delete secure sandboxes, getting all of those same optimizations with no infrastructure to manage.

295
00:34:43,022 --> 00:34:46,916
But what if your priority is customization and multi-cloud portability?

296
00:34:47,694 --> 00:34:57,892
You still need isolation, rapid activation, and statefulness, but you want to manage those environments across your own Kubernetes clusters, whether on Google Kubernetes Engine, on-prem, or multiple clouds.

297
00:34:58,606 --> 00:35:04,836
That's why we built Agent Substrate, an open source, secure by default agent execution runtime.

298
00:35:05,998 --> 00:35:11,140
You can orchestrate agent sandboxes using the standard Kubernetes APIs you already know.

299
00:35:11,630 --> 00:35:23,684
You get hardware level container sandboxing, micro VM support, sub-second lifecycle control, and automated state persistence, all integrated directly with your existing observability, networking, and deployment pipelines.

300
00:35:25,134 --> 00:35:32,548
We also know that the agentic era is moving far too fast for any single company to solve all of those infrastructure challenges behind closed doors.

301
00:35:33,294 --> 00:35:36,676
This transition demands open, community-driven innovation.

302
00:35:38,094 --> 00:35:44,964
Ten years ago, Google created an open-sourced Kubernetes, which became the gold standard for cloud native runtimes.

303
00:35:45,934 --> 00:35:48,612
The industry needs another Kubernetes moment.

304
00:35:49,070 --> 00:35:52,612
It needs an open foundation for agent native runtimes.

305
00:35:53,838 --> 00:36:01,444
That's why today I'm proud to announce that Google is formally donating Agent Substrate to the Cloud Native Computing Foundation.

306
00:36:02,798 --> 00:36:09,956
Just as we did with Kubernetes, we're putting our internal Google capabilities into the hands of the community to drive open innovation and standardization.

307
00:36:10,574 --> 00:36:17,316
As agent fleets grow in size, complexity, and run anywhere, keeping them secure and scalable requires robust governance.

308
00:36:17,870 --> 00:36:23,204
That's why we recently announced a consistent governance model for agents running across environments.

309
00:36:23,694 --> 00:36:30,404
At its center is Agent Gateway, serving as the air traffic controller for all client-to-agent and agent-to-anywhere traffic.

310
00:36:31,086 --> 00:36:36,164
Working alongside it, Agent Registry provides a central directory of approved tools and MCP servers.

311
00:36:37,134 --> 00:36:42,276
Agent Identity gives each agent a digital signature for access control and auditing.

312
00:36:42,926 --> 00:36:46,948
And agent observability captures the full telemetry across every interaction.

313
00:36:48,046 --> 00:36:56,676
And with token management embedded directly in the gateway, you can attribute spend, enforce budget guardrails, and eliminate the exposure of secrets or clear text tokens to agents.

314
00:36:57,134 --> 00:37:02,596
Together, these capabilities give you complete, unified governance across clouds and on-prem.

315
00:37:03,278 --> 00:37:09,764
So, these are powerful software and orchestration capabilities designed specifically for the needs of agentic execution.

316
00:37:10,318 --> 00:37:14,096
But ultimately, these need to run on an actual CPU hardware platform.

317
00:37:14,414 --> 00:37:19,236
And just like for every other workload, we don't believe in one size fits all here either.

318
00:37:19,726 --> 00:37:26,500
Different types of agentic workloads will require different types of CPU platforms that are optimized for their unique needs.

319
00:37:27,694 --> 00:37:34,372
So we built Agent Compute Engine and Agent Substrate to run across our broad portfolio of CPU instances in Google Cloud.

320
00:37:35,342 --> 00:37:41,444
That starts with our deep partnerships with Intel and AMD, giving you the latest generation of standard x86 processors.

321
00:37:41,806 --> 00:37:47,268
And alongside x86, we're optimizing our custom ARM based processors, Axion, for the agentic era.

322
00:37:48,206 --> 00:37:54,596
In fact, our Axion N4A instances deliver up to 30% better price performance than the next leading hyperscaler.

323
00:37:55,118 --> 00:37:59,972
And this is backed by high speed RDMA networking for 40% higher throughput.

324
00:38:01,390 --> 00:38:14,116
When you step back and look at everything we shared today, you see a fundamentally different approach to computing with end-to-end capabilities for agentic inference and execution that can help you move from promising demos to production at scale.

325
00:38:15,374 --> 00:38:20,772
You see that impact in the mission-critical AI applications that are powered by the AI hypercomputer every day.

326
00:38:21,934 --> 00:38:26,500
We're enabling a wide range of companies and developers to build our next great idea on our infrastructure.

327
00:38:27,342 --> 00:38:37,060
First, across the large-scale enterprises, global leaders in banking, financial services, healthcare, and telecom are deploying agents across their organizations through our Gemini platform.

328
00:38:37,934 --> 00:38:47,108
Second, across digital natives, fast-growing companies in online retail, entertainment, and commerce are building their own custom agents and platforms directly on our infrastructure.

329
00:38:47,854 --> 00:38:58,180
And third, across the top AI labs in the world who rely on our foundational hardware and high-speed networking to train and serve the next generation of frontier intelligence.

330
00:38:59,278 --> 00:39:02,308
You should never have to compromise between performance and choice.

331
00:39:02,702 --> 00:39:04,548
And on Google Cloud, you don't have to.

332
00:39:05,550 --> 00:39:11,520
Across every layer of our stack, we introduce critical new capabilities built specifically for the demands of the agentic era.

333
00:39:11,920 --> 00:39:16,000
TPUs and GPUs designed for the emerging demands of agentic inference.

334
00:39:16,240 --> 00:39:23,120
Optimize and open source software to maximize token throughput and keep your accelerators running at peak efficiency.

335
00:39:23,520 --> 00:39:35,120
And execution runtimes that are built for agents, giving you turnkey micro VM sandboxing in the cloud, open source and control on Kubernetes, and consistent cross cloud governance.

336
00:39:35,360 --> 00:39:38,000
The platform is ready, the tools are in your hands.

337
00:39:38,240 --> 00:39:41,680
We're really looking forward to helping you bring your boldest ideas to life.

338
00:39:41,920 --> 00:39:44,160
Thank you so much for joining us today.

